@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-function-comparators Function Comparators
@brief Functions for comparing actual and expected values in tests.

@details
While any boolean expression can be used in expectations and requirements,
dedicated comparator functions such as `eq`, `ne`, `lt`, `le`, `gt`, and
`ge` provide enhanced reporting by explicitly showing the compared values
when a check fails.

Using these comparators ensures that failed checks report both actual and
expected values, making issues easier to diagnose.

Comparators are available for both fundamental types and standard
containers. When a container comparison fails, the elements of both
containers are shown.

@par Examples

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check various conditions", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::eq (compute_answer (), 42)) << "answer is 42";
  t.expect (mt::ne (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer is not 43";
  t.expect (mt::lt (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer is < 43";
  t.expect (mt::le (compute_answer (), 43)) << "answer is <= 43";
  t.expect (mt::gt (compute_answer (), 41)) << "answer is > 41";
  t.expect (mt::ge (compute_answer (), 42)) << "answer is >= 42";

  t.expect (compute_condition ()) << "condition is true";
});
@endcode

If a comparator check fails, the output clearly indicates the actual and
expected values:

@code{.console}
  Check failed comparisons
    ✗ actual != 42 FAILED (unit-test.cpp:286, 42 != 42)
    ✗ FAILED (unit-test.cpp:307, 42 != 42)
    ✗ 42 != 42_i FAILED (unit-test.cpp:310, 42 != 42)
    ✗ (actual == 42) and (actual != 42.0) FAILED (unit-test.cpp:781, (42 == 42
and 42.000000 != 42.000000))
@endcode

The C strings (`char` arrays, including the string literals, and `char`
pointers) are compared by content, also with `std::string` and
`std::string_view`, so `mt::eq (actual, "abc")` checks the characters,
not the addresses; a null `char` pointer is equal only to another null
pointer (or `nullptr`), not to an empty string. The other pointers and
arrays (including `uint8_t` buffers) are compared by address.

@par Comparing Containers

Containers can be compared for equality or inequality using the same
comparator functions. The comparison is performed with the standard
`==` operator, so the containers must be of the same type. There are no
`==` and `!=` operators for containers in the `operators` namespace,
since the standard ones would be selected, and these return a plain
`bool`.

@par Examples

@code{.cpp}
ts.test ("Check containers", [] (auto& t) {
  t.expect (mt::eq (std::vector<int>{ 1, 2 }, std::vector<int>{ 1, 2 }))
      << "vector{ 1, 2 } eq vector{ 1, 2 }";

  t.expect (mt::ne (std::vector<int>{ 1, 2, 3 },
                    std::vector<int>{ 1, 2, 4 }))
      << "vector{ 1, 2, 3 } ne vector{ 1, 2, 4 }";
});
@endcode

